<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Migraine without aura on propranolol (G43.009)</content>. Propranolol 80 mg is well tolerated, with a pulse of 60 and blood pressure of 116/74. Migraine days are down from 13 to 4 in four weeks, but part of the fall is the natural end of the bout, and it is too early to judge the preventive. The triptan with naproxen works well. Acute medicine on 4 days, within the limits. The PHQ-9 planned four to six weeks after starting propranolol was not repeated today. [S]<list><item>Increase propranolol to 120 mg each evening.</item><item>Continue sumatriptan with naproxen as needed.</item><item>Three-month review with the MIDAS, HIT-6 and PHQ-9.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Stiff right wrist after an old dislocation (M25.631)</content>. Still waiting for occupational therapy. [S]<list><item>The clinic has booked a hand therapist with an earlier opening.</item><item>Dictation for long reports.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Red-flag screen (SNNOOP10): systemic signs, neurological signs, sudden or new pattern, onset after 50, positional or Valsalva features, papilledema, progressive course</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No red flags</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Headache diary: headache and migraine days, attack features, triggers</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>4 migraine days in four weeks; three days missing during the cold</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Acute-medicine days (medication overuse headache limits: triptan fewer than 10, NSAID fewer than 15 days a month)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Acute medicine on 4 days in four weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Acute treatment response (pain-free at two hours, return to work)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Pain-free at two hours in 3 of 4 attacks with sumatriptan and naproxen</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Propranolol tolerability: pulse, blood pressure, tiredness, exercise tolerance, wheeze, cold hands, sleep</td><td>Four weeks after starting or changing the dose, then each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Pulse 60, blood pressure 116/74; tiredness in the first week only; no wheeze; dose raised to 120 mg</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Preventive efficacy (at least 50% fewer migraine days)</td><td>At two to three months on an adequate dose</td><td>Due</td><td>Four weeks on 80 mg; efficacy judged at the three-month review</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Disability (MIDAS, recorded as its five items, and HIT-6)</td><td>At baseline and after about three months of prevention</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Repeat at three months</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood (PHQ-9) and anxiety (GAD-7), including on a beta-blocker</td><td>At baseline, four to six weeks after starting a preventive that can affect mood, then at each review</td><td>Deferred</td><td>The PHQ-9 planned four to six weeks after starting propranolol was not repeated today. No harm is evident: he reports a steady mood and no low mood or loss of interest on direct questions. Corrective action: PHQ-2 at the preventive visit and PHQ-9 and GAD-7 at the three-month review</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Neuroimaging</td><td>Only for red flags or a change in pattern</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No red flags or change in pattern</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Sleep, meals, caffeine and work-stress plan</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Fixed wake time and regular lunch kept; walks most days</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Occupational therapy visit for the right wrist and writing</td><td>Within four weeks of intake</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Not seen four weeks after intake because of a waiting list. No harm so far beyond continued slow writing, eased by dictation. Corrective action: the clinic booked a hand therapist with an earlier opening</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>A two-week headache diary that allows a firm diagnosis. Outcome by 1790-05-21: Diary of 14 days kept; ICHD-3 criteria for migraine without aura met; no red flags [S]</item><item>Monthly migraine days reduced by at least half within three months of starting prevention. In progress; target 1790-08-13 [S]</item><item>Acute medicine on no more than two days a week, so that medication overuse headache does not develop. In progress; target 1790-08-13 [S]</item><item>Pain-free within two hours of acute treatment in most attacks. In progress; target 1790-08-13 [S]</item><item>Lower headache-related disability. In progress; target 1790-08-13 [S]</item><item>Write for longer without wrist pain. In progress; target 1790-08-13 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Headache Classification Committee of the International Headache Society (IHS). The International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (2018); The American Headache Society Consensus Statement: Update on integrating new migraine treatments into clinical practice (2021); Calcitonin gene-related peptide-targeting therapies are a first-line option for the prevention of migraine: An American Headache Society position statement update (2024); The American Headache Society Position Statement On Integrating New Migraine Treatments Into Clinical Practice (2019); Pharmacologic Treatments of Acute Episodic Migraine Headache in Outpatient Settings: A Clinical Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2025); Prevention of Episodic Migraine Headache Using Pharmacologic Treatments in Outpatient Settings: A Clinical Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2025); Choosing wisely in headache medicine: the American Headache Society&apos;s list of five things physicians and patients should question (2013); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive visit in four weeks. Three-month review in eight weeks. Diary continued. Next scheduled visit 1790-07-16: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
